my name is lauren price i'm a networking  specialist customer engineer at google  cloud and today i'm going to talk to you  about the networking concepts for cloud  data fusion that are helpful to know for  implementation security and connectivity  [Music]  cloud data fusion is google cloud's  native managed service for enterprise  data integration and ingestion  it provides a visual point and click  interface enabling a code-free  deployment of etl and elt data pipelines  cloud data fusion has a number of  components that exist behind the scenes  that power the service and it creates a  tenant project and vpc to hold those  resources  the data fusion components that are  surfaced to the user are the data fusion  instance and the data proc clusters  each cloud data fusion instance contains  a unique independent cloud data fusion  deployment that contains a set of  services that handle pipeline lifecycle  management orchestration coordination  and metadata management  cloud data fusion creates ephemeral  execution environments to run pipelines  when you manually run your pipelines or  when pipelines run through a time  schedule or a pipeline state trigger  cloud data fusion uses dataproc as an  execution environment in which you can  choose to run pipelines as spark or  spark streaming programs cloud data  fusion provisions an ephemeral dataproc  cluster in the user project at the  beginning of a pipeline run  executes the pipeline using mapreduce or  spark in the cluster and then deletes  the cluster after the pipeline execution  is complete  alternatively if you manage your  dataproc clusters in controlled  environments you can also configure  cloud data fusion not to provision  ephemeral clusters and run pipelines  against existing proc clusters instead  the cloud data fusion instance can be  thought of as the design environment and  the dataproc cluster as the execution  environment  there are two types of cloud data fusion  instances based on an access model a  public instance which is the default  deployment and a private instance  let's dig into the access patterns from  different scenarios  we can start with the public ip  deployment pattern  in a public instance network  communication happens over the open  internet regardless of the source  location which is not recommended for  critical environments  the cloud data fusion instance for the  design environment will have a public ip  however the data proc cluster used for  the execution environment can be  deployed with either public ip or  private ips  let's move on to the private ip  deployment pattern  cloud data fusion private ip has  different setup steps depending on  whether you are using a standard vpc or  a shared vpc network  if you're using a standard vpc you can  create the instance using either the  cloud console or a curl command however  if you are using a shared vpc you must  use a curl command check out the  documentation that's linked in the  description for more information when  private ip instances are created a  separate tenant project is created for  each user project in which cloud data  fusion instances are provisioned  additionally cloud data fusion private  ip requires a peering connection to  communicate privately with sources  located in the user vpc and is required  for the design environment capabilities  this parent connection must be created  manually  if you need to access a public source  using a private ip cloud data fusion  instance when designing pipelines you  must use version 6.4 or above  in the design environment cloud data  fusion traffic will traverse a vpc pier  from the tenant vpc to the user vpc  traffic will then be directed to an  internal load balancer fronting a  managed instance group of gateway vms  traffic will egress through the gateway  vms through a cloud nat configured for  the subnet and to the public source on  the internet  the dataproc cluster used for the  execution environment should be  configured to egress through the  cloudnet as well to reach the public  source  for sources that are hosted within the  uservpc cloud data fusion will connect  over a vpc pier  firewalls must be configured in the  source vpc to allow the source to  communicate with the cloud data fusion  instance as well as the dataproc  execution environment  if you are in a peered hub and spoke  architecture model and you are trying to  make a connection from a vpc that is not  directly peered to the cloud data fusion  tenant vpc you must use a proxy due to  the non-transitive routing nature of  google's vpcs  this will only be needed for the design  environment  be aware you can also replace a peering  hub and spoke architecture with a vpn  hub and spoke model so that the cloud  data fusion 10 and vpc route can be  advertised over the  vpn non-transitive peering also comes  into play when trying to connect to a  google cloud source that is deployed  using another tenant project for example  cloud sql  cloud data fusion and cloud sql are both  hosted in tenant projects but these  projects are different in order for  cloud data fusion to connect to a cloud  sql source a proxy vm must be deployed  in the user vpc to proxy traffic to the  cloud sql instance when using the design  environment since the data proc clusters  used in the execution environment are  hosted in the user vpc they will be able  to connect to cloud sql without the use  of the proxy  when accessing sources in an on-prem  environment with cloud data fusion  private ip a hybrid connection must  exist between the user vpc and the on  prime environment  either an havpn or interconnect can be  used both the ipsider range for the  tenant vpc where the cloud data fusion  instance is hosted as well as the ip  citer range of the dataproc cluster in  the uservpc must be advertised over the  hybrid connection and on-prem firewalls  must be configured to accept ingress  from these ranges  if the vlan or vpn lands in another  peered vpc then you'll need to use a  proxy to connect the source to the cloud  data fusion instance  when accessing data sources both public  and private instances make outgoing  calls to google cloud apis using private  google access let's briefly cover some  security topics  for cloud data fusion versions below 6.5  you can only manage access at an  instance level if you have access to an  instance you have access to all  pipelines and metadata in that instance  for version 6.5 and above you also have  the ability to use role-based access  control to establish name-space level  isolation within a single cloud data  fusion instance this feature is  currently in preview  pipeline access to data is provided by  granting access to the service account  which can be a custom service account  that you specify  you must make sure that the service  account has proper iam permissions in  place to access any google cloud-hosted  data sources  for a pipeline execution you control  ingress and egress by setting the  appropriate firewall rules on the user  vpc on which the pipeline is being  executed  before we wrap up let's summarize the  three most important takeaways from  today  cloud data fusion exists as a design  environment that runs within a tenant  project in vpc and an execution  environment that runs as a dataproc  cluster within the uservpc  cloud data fusion can be launched with a  public ip or private ip and vpc peering  must manually be configured between the  tenant bpc and user vpc for private ip  firewall rules must be configured to  allow connections to and from both the  cloud data fusion instance citer and the  dataproc citer  thanks for watching and look out for  another video on the concepts of  networking for google cloud's managed  services  [Music]  you
 

